<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">progression</ansi>

How your character gets better. There are no levels and no experience
points in Gaius. You improve the things you actually use.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The One Rule ━━━</ansi>

Every time you use an attribute or a skill, you get one chance to
improve it. Not a little bit of progress toward a bar. A chance.

Most of those chances come to nothing. Some of them land, and when one
lands you see a banner naming what improved.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ What Makes It Harder ━━━</ansi>

The further something has already come, the rarer that chance becomes.

That is the whole difficulty curve. A skill you have barely touched
improves quickly and visibly. One you have pushed a long way improves
rarely, and keeps improving rarely rather than stopping outright. There
is no ceiling you slam into. There is no point where a thing can no
longer improve at all.

What does <ansi fg="red">not</ansi> matter:

  <ansi fg="stat">How many times you have done it.</ansi> Swinging a sword ten thousand
  times does not make the next swing teach you less. Only how far the
  skill has come counts.

  <ansi fg="stat">Your equipment.</ansi> Gear that raises an attribute does not make that
  attribute harder to train. Only what you have earned counts.

  <ansi fg="stat">How big or old your character is.</ansi> A fresh attribute is cheap for
  everyone.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ How To Improve Something Specific ━━━</ansi>

Use it. That is genuinely the whole answer, but the useful version is
knowing what trains what:

  <ansi fg="stat">Strength</ansi>      Hitting things in melee.
  <ansi fg="stat">Dexterity</ansi>     Attacking, dodging and parrying. It fires constantly,
                so each chance is deliberately smaller.
  <ansi fg="stat">Perception</ansi>    Foraging, searching, spotting what is hidden, aimed
                shots, and the crafts that need a careful eye.
  <ansi fg="stat">Vitality</ansi>      Taking a solid hit, and recovering afterwards. A
                glancing blow you mostly turned aside will not do it.
  <ansi fg="stat">Willpower</ansi>     Casting, and holding a spell together when you are
                struck.
  <ansi fg="stat">Charisma</ansi>      Taunting, haggling, and manifestation.

Skills train from the obvious thing: fighting with a weapon trains
weapon-combat, brewing trains alchemy, studying a corpse trains
manifestation. See <ansi fg="command">help skills</ansi> for the full list.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Doing Something Exceptional ━━━</ansi>

A critical success teaches you more than a routine one, and so does a
fumble. Failure is instructive. Being on the receiving end of either
teaches you something too, though less than causing it.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Where To See It ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">status</ansi>   your attributes, described in words
  <ansi fg="command">skills</ansi>   your skills and their tiers

Both describe where you are rather than printing raw numbers, which is
deliberate. The feel of a thing is what you play with.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ A Note On Companions ━━━</ansi>

Creatures bound to you improve the same way you do, within limits. A
companion that has already come a very long way may have stopped
improving. It keeps everything it has earned. See <ansi fg="command">help companion</ansi>.

